 @lasarith2  It's almost certainly the case. I don't, however, see that as a problem. There's such a shortage of cards right now, these are still going to be great for most people, and will get more cards on the market to alleviate pressure.

I'll also mention that it's not very useful to talk about ray tracing on these cards. You don't buy AMD for ray tracing. Ironically, you can already see new shaders in modern game engines that can keep up with the visual fidelity of ray tracing anyway, so the relevance of ray tracing for a great gaming experience is, frankly, of questionable importance anyway.

This card is cheaper to make, takes less fabrication or can use partially failed parts, is appealing to consumers but less so for scalpers, and ignoring ray tracing, performs well today, and will likely age gracefully.

 @Jojo-nq3bp  No they’re not. And they won’t for a while, because the next gen consoles have the performance of a 2080 and they won’t design games that are beyond the requirements of next gen consoles.

For reference, a 1080Ti is roughly at 2080 performance while a 5700XT is closer to a 2070S, both of which are still fine cards to have in the current day and age. They will be good enough on 1080/1440p for a few more years.

In fact, the 1650 is still good enough to play most modern titles on medium/high settings at 60fps on 1080.

